  keywords     = {aesthetic experience},
  abstract     = {{Accounts of the fates of film theory after 1970
                  often, rightly, articulate a rupture between
                  psychoanalytic film theory and its successors,
                  especially the \enquote{turn to affect.} This
                  chapter describes, across this rupture, a shared
                  intellectual problem that animates both: that of the
                  impersonality of experience. This chapter narrates
                  the shift (paraphrasing Eugenie Brinkema) from the
                  episteme of the spectator to the episteme of affect
                  by focusing on how impersonality structures both. In
                  so doing, it emphasizes film theory's basic project
                  of sorting out what is personal and impersonal in
                  our encounters with cinema; part of this project
                  involves developing vocabulary to describe and
                  theorize about the technical articulations and
                  intimate structures of our horizons of experience.
                  The history of film theory---the history of how we
                  speak to one another about what is meaningful in
                  movies and our experiences of them---is, then, one
                  expression of the history of experience. Film theory
                  both in its psychoanalytical phase and its
                  subsequent turn toward affect remains a crucial
                  intellectual inheritance and scholarly practice for
                  reckoning with our present and the ways technical
                  media structure our experience. In other words, the
                  history of film theory since 1970 can be usefully
                  understood as a series of schemes for articulating
                  the intimate impersonality of our relations to
                  technology. It is thus also a history of ways of
                  articulating the political import of what's personal
                  and impersonal about experience in the cinema, and
                  our experience with technical media more broadly.}},
  date-added   = {2025-08-24 17:55:42 +0300},
